<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deva Raya II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1424">1424</a>–<a href="/years/1446">1446</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangama-dynasty">Sangama dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deva Raya II was emperor of Vijayanagara of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangama-dynasty">Sangama dynasty</a> from 1424 to 1446, under whom the empire reached a high point. He reformed the army, recruiting Muslim cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and the Gajapatis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and received the Persian envoy Abdur Razzaq, who described the splendour of the capital. He was a patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deva-raya-ii.jpg" alt="Deva Raya II" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: N/A · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Devaraya_II_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Deva Raya II succeeded his father Veera Vijaya Bukka Raya after a short two-year reign. As crown prince he had already defeated the Bahamani Sultanate, which moved its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> in about 1426. He fought the Gajapati of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> three times: against Bhanudeva IV at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondavidu">Kondavidu</a> in about 1427, against Kapilendra when he tried to take Rajamahendri in about 1436, and again in about 1441. An invasion by the Reddis of Kondavidu was repulsed, and by about 1432 the petty chiefs of the region had been brought under Vijayanagara control.</p><p>Against the Bahamanis the results were mixed. When Ala-ud-din II took the throne in about 1436, Deva Raya II, according to the historian Sastri, had to pay a large tribute for peace, and repeated defeats led him to enlist skilled Muslim soldiers. Abdur Razzak, who was present, wrote that in about 1443 a brother of the king beheaded nobles at a feast and stabbed and wounded the king himself. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> then demanded 700,000 pagodas; Deva Raya II refused, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> and Bankapura, but was pushed back to Mudgal, and lands in the doab between the Tungabhadra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rivers were lost. His commander Lakkanna invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> and collected tribute there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Quilon</a> was defeated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The king himself is credited with the romantic stories Sobagina Sone, The Drizzle of Beauty, and Amaruka in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and the Mahanataka Sudhanidhi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. His court saw fierce rivalry between Vaishnava and Veerashaiva writers. It included the Kannada poets Chamarasa and Kumara Vyasa, Chandrashekara, who wrote on secular topics, and the Veerashaiva ministers and writers Lakkana Dandesa and Jakkanarya. He supported the mathematician Parameshvara of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> school of astronomy and mathematics.</p><p>The Telugu poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinatha">Srinatha</a>, who defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> scholar Dindima in debate, received the title Kavisarvabhauma, Emperor of poets, and the king honoured him with a kanakabhisheka, a shower of gold coins on the head. The European explorer Nicolo Conti judged the king more powerful than all the other kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and Razzak described a capital enclosed by seven walls and citadels, with jewellers selling pearls, rubies, emeralds and diamonds openly in the bazaar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deva Raya II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Deva Raya II</b> (r. 1424–1446) recruited Muslim cavalry and archers and gave them land, aiming to match the mounted archery of his Deccani rivals, and firearms were adopted in the sixteenth century, sometimes with Portuguese help.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
